othyroxine) started after certain treatments to keep TSH low or normal; may not always be necessary after lobectomy because remaining thyroid tissue can produce hormones.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>LEVOTHYROXINE<|>object<|>A synthetic thyroid hormone used for hormone replacement therapy and TSH suppression/normalization; may be given after treatment or during watch-and-wait monitoring depending on clinical circumstances.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>THYROID-STIMULATING HORMONE (TSH)<|>object<|>A blood-measured hormone used to assess thyroid hormone status and guide levothyroxine dosing; clinicians aim to keep it low or normal in some thyroid cancer contexts; used in routine follow-up after both lobectomy and total thyroidectomy.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>RADIOACTIVE IODINE THERAPY (RAI THERAPY)<|>activity<|>A treatment option after total thyroidectomy (when all/most cancer is removed) used to kill remaining thyroid cancer cells; recommended based on risk factors such as spread beyond thyroid, blood vessel invasion, high Tg, or extensive lymph node involvement.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>WHOLE-BODY RAI IMAGING<|>activity<|>A post-RAI imaging procedure to look for residual or recurrent cancer; may be helpful for high-risk patients, iodine-avid metastases, or abnormal blood/ultrasound results.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>PHYSICAL EXAM<|>activity<|>Clinical examination used in short-term and long-term follow-up after thyroid cancer surgery (both total thyroidectomy and lobectomy) as part of surveillance for recurrence.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>BLOOD TESTS<|>activity<|>Laboratory testing used for surveillance; after total thyroidectomy commonly includes TSH, thyroglobulin (Tg), and anti-thyroglobulin antibodies (Tg ab); after lobectomy includes TSH testing, while Tg testing is noted as not helpful.)##\n(\"entity\"<|>NECK ULTRASOUND<|>activity<|>Imaging test recommended 6–12 months after surgery and periodically thereafter to monitor for thyroid cancer recurrence in the neck; frequency should be discussed with the care team.